1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:09,360
Sam, Brian mentioned that you have um uh that it's been quite a summer for mathematics already.

2
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:12,868
So, where are we going with mathematics and AI?

3
00:00:14,126 --> 00:00:21,412
Yes, as Professor Brian Schmidt properly mentioned, we are going through a turmoil and great change.

4
00:00:22,222 --> 00:00:32,836
Some change that we have never seen in the past 5,000 year history of mathematics, because mathematics was very different from other fields of science.

5
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:38,372
And when Thomas Kuhn's scientific revolution theory, it wasn't mathematics.

6
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:49,380
We always have the same paradigms that we want to prove great theorems, in other words, make great discoveries and have a good understanding of it.

7
00:00:49,902 --> 00:00:53,060
So that is the 5,000 years of history of mathematics.

8
00:00:53,870 --> 00:01:01,860
It could look useless at first, but after a few hundred years or thousand years, it became extremely useful for the human race.

9
00:01:02,606 --> 00:01:05,732
But in the first time in the history, these two are diverging.

10
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:08,164
Understanding and discoveries.

11
00:01:08,878 --> 00:01:09,956
So so far it is aligned.

12
00:01:10,222 --> 00:01:14,788
So if you make great discoveries, that means you're understanding something very well.

13
00:01:15,342 --> 00:01:16,388
And that is good for mathematics.

14
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:20,900
But now we are flooded by discoveries produced by AI.

15
00:01:21,262 --> 00:01:25,796
Sometimes they have errors, but I'm sure that the errors will be less and less common.

16
00:01:26,318 --> 00:01:30,660
AIs will get better, and their answers, their discoveries will be more and more correct.

17
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:39,396
But then mathematicians realize that we are having too much discoveries and much less understanding.

18
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:40,960
That's what is happening.

19
00:01:43,086 --> 00:01:44,720
So that's the current situation.

20
00:01:45,166 --> 00:01:52,640
It's a concept that would surprise the public at large that you can have discovery without understanding, but it's something that scientists very much understand.

21
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:53,040
Yeah.

22
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:54,480
Yeah, very sad.

23
00:01:55,182 --> 00:02:06,720
Craig, I mean you you discovered RNA interference back in well, back in the day and got the Nobel Prize in 2006, but you're still trying to unravel information flow in biology.

24
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:08,644
Understanding takes time.

25
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:14,948
Well, it's interesting that you know, in 5,000 years you're now seeing this divergence.

26
00:02:15,566 --> 00:02:20,400
I think with science, that divergence has always existed.

27
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:33,120
In fact, with the scientific enterprise discovery, in my experience, deepens your lack of understanding.

28
00:02:33,294 --> 00:02:38,880
It brings your, it makes you appreciate more how little you understand.

29
00:02:40,206 --> 00:02:42,800
and I think Brian's discovery is a great example.

30
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:48,240
I mean, not that I understand cosmology, but how can the universe be accelerating?

31
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:55,940
I mean, it just does anyone understand gravity and yet it's a reality in our daily lives.

32
00:02:57,134 --> 00:02:59,556
I kind of love that about science.

33
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:10,500
And I think mathematics is so different because you live within sort of a theoretical realm that's created by the numbers.

34
00:03:11,054 --> 00:03:17,476
Whereas science, we live in this inexplicable universe that none of us can understand.

35
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:21,508
and by the way, that goes for the AI too.

36
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:26,400
So, in a way, I feel like maybe it's not so bad.

37
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:34,240
Will the AI have an existential crisis like I did growing up?

38
00:03:36,110 --> 00:03:38,500
Teglan, you're a material scientist.

39
00:03:39,406 --> 00:03:41,488
What is AI doing to material science?

40
00:03:41,648 --> 00:03:43,204
What is it going to do to material science?

41
00:03:44,078 --> 00:03:47,556
Actually, I've been working on material discovery for the last 30 years.

42
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:52,196
And as you might know, I think it's in every area, in particular materials area.

43
00:03:52,688 --> 00:03:59,300
I mean, the process of like a new discovery of materials really speed up by these AI tools.

44
00:03:59,790 --> 00:04:09,408
And we can also work on the humongous large number of materials candidate, And we can just using AI, just gonna select the most promising one.

45
00:04:09,742 --> 00:04:18,660
So instead of like, I mean, the doing the experimental calculation one material by one material, you know, as we did I mean, you know, old times.

46
00:04:19,022 --> 00:04:22,500
So we can actually focus on the first like using AI.

47
00:04:22,702 --> 00:04:35,568
Just like, I mean, the I mean get the most promising materials candidate groups, and we can just focus on that, and we can just do a lot of like I mean the calculations and the experiment on that.

48
00:04:35,918 --> 00:04:39,088
So you I think you have one of the largest groups of scientists in Korea.

49
00:04:39,168 --> 00:04:39,728
You have a huge group.

50
00:04:40,768 --> 00:04:43,408
And you have about 20% focused on AI research.

51
00:04:44,208 --> 00:04:45,328
directed research at the moment.

52
00:04:45,742 --> 00:04:49,088
Do you find that that 20% is going faster than the other 80%?

53
00:04:49,968 --> 00:05:01,540
Actually, you know, what happened is actually the way I do is I mean, they do collaborate with the rest of like about 30% of like our group is actually working on the AI-driven materials discovery.

54
00:05:01,808 --> 00:05:04,848
And actually they are working on the rest of like the rest of 70%.

55
00:05:05,422 --> 00:05:07,044
You know, they have to collaborate.

56
00:05:07,342 --> 00:05:12,644
And for example, I mean, you know, we try to develop new kind of catalyst for the hydrogen production.

57
00:05:13,248 --> 00:05:24,068
And then they're kind of like I mean, the new machine learning tools can be actually translated to the even to the like discovery of some kind of new drug delivery vehicle.

58
00:05:24,366 --> 00:05:25,828
It looks like totally different, right?

59
00:05:26,094 --> 00:05:28,199
That's actually fun part of doing research, right?

60
00:05:28,302 --> 00:05:37,488
It Looks totally different when you know the when when you understand something really important in one area, it's easy to translate to the other area.

61
00:05:37,568 --> 00:05:39,648
That's fun of doing science in general.

62
00:05:39,808 --> 00:05:40,068
Yeah.

63
00:05:41,102 --> 00:05:45,088
Ava is also a material scientist, so you share this in common.

64
00:05:45,678 --> 00:05:50,180
Would you like to tackle that same question of where how is AI going to transform your science?

65
00:05:51,168 --> 00:05:53,568
So I see two main things.

66
00:05:53,838 --> 00:05:58,180
So take one is making materials, I'm studying the materials.

67
00:05:58,448 --> 00:06:05,248
I see where the atoms are going within the materials, and sometimes they don't do exactly what we think they should be doing.

68
00:06:05,968 --> 00:06:07,908
And that is giving us new possibilities.

69
00:06:08,462 --> 00:06:14,688
What I see with AI, and this is happening already, is that it has an enormous capacity to handle information.

70
00:06:15,648 --> 00:06:21,348
And it also gives us a possibility of doing evaluation in real time.

71
00:06:21,806 --> 00:06:26,820
I'm using, and we are using, electron microscopes where we actually look at the atoms.

72
00:06:27,406 --> 00:06:34,468
And sometimes in the old days, we had to do data evaluation after having done the microscopy.

73
00:06:34,768 --> 00:06:40,004
But nowadays we sit there and with the help of AI we do the real-time evaluation.

74
00:06:40,368 --> 00:06:44,836
So the progress is much, much quicker.

75
00:06:45,728 --> 00:06:48,356
You gain knowledge, much more efficient.

76
00:06:49,422 --> 00:07:00,260
And the other thing that I see, and this is more that is coming for future, is that we are used to work in a modular fashion, step by step gaining knowledge.

77
00:07:01,742 --> 00:07:15,396
What I see with AI is like having a sphere with a lot of information, but also data, but also information about the laws of physics and what is happening in chemistry.

78
00:07:15,982 --> 00:07:31,844
And what we can do is simply put in our question, and something is happening within our sphere, and we get out an answer that is not typical for our modular thinking, but something that actually can look very different and be very inspiring for us.

79
00:07:42,126 --> 00:07:48,080
It's up to us to make it inspiring because as you were saying before.

80
00:07:48,366 --> 00:07:48,708
Yeah.

81
00:07:49,646 --> 00:07:49,988
Ah yes.

82
00:07:52,046 --> 00:07:52,516
Thank you.

83
00:07:53,280 --> 00:08:05,860
As you were saying before, we get new ideas, but sometimes we actually don't understand what that new idea is about, or if it's really something that has to do with reality.

84
00:08:06,446 --> 00:08:14,180
So we has to have we have to have a basic knowledge, a basic understanding to be able to understand what AI has provided us with.

85
00:08:15,182 --> 00:08:18,948
And that's a challenge for future and especially our young generation.

86
00:08:22,318 --> 00:08:24,320
Our generation, yeah, that's right.

87
00:08:25,390 --> 00:08:26,160
Sam, do you want to come in?

88
00:08:26,478 --> 00:08:31,876
Oh yes, so I mean history is already moving.

89
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:34,532
I have no intention of denying it.

90
00:08:34,990 --> 00:08:39,044
So we'll have to either adjust it or just quit what you're doing.

91
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:52,720
But the question that is recurring to me and to my colleagues is then in this case, if you value discoveries over understanding, do we really need a human in the loop?

92
00:08:53,520 --> 00:09:00,100
So that is the question that I sometimes have some part-time working with Google in mind, AI company.

93
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:05,092
And so originally you wanted to build some great co mathematician.

94
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:12,964
Mathematicians want to solve certain problems, and AI can suggest certain directions and change what we are doing and also drafting.

95
00:09:13,806 --> 00:09:22,800
But eventually we realized that are humans really needed, or are we bottlenecks if you consider automated labs?

96
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:25,828
for instance, if you want to just make discoveries, for instance.

97
00:09:32,974 --> 00:09:34,788
So, what's their view of this, do you think?

98
00:09:35,662 --> 00:09:39,040
Well, so we wanted to solve great problems, actually.

99
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:52,800
So, well, their purpose is a little different, of course, but the purpose is they want to check the limit of AI model if you're given with almost unlimited source of resources, especially computing resources.

100
00:09:53,134 --> 00:09:56,292
So they want to make benchmarks and check their limits.

101
00:09:57,070 --> 00:10:04,324
But the participating mathematicians have their own agendas of solving great problems with their names on it.

102
00:10:04,814 --> 00:10:28,420
But we are realizing that if you just let the AI discover, like the recent announcement by OpenAI about Navier Stokes problem, or other Claude announcement of Jacobian conjecture, if let these things just prevail, the media, overhype, and also young generations of mathematics.

103
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:36,320
then maybe people start to have a misconception about mathematics, and then humans will be buttleg.

104
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:37,764
and let AI do everything.

105
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:42,640
Because one has to remember, actually, this is not what I said.

106
00:10:42,720 --> 00:11:06,640
There is a very inspiring article by great scientist novelist Ted Chang, who wrote to Nature, the science journal, in 2000, depicting a future that humans are always behind AI in scientific discoveries, and nature is publishing only articles by AI, not from humans anymore.

107
00:11:06,926 --> 00:11:13,508
But then the closing remark of Teddy Chang was but you should remember who made this AI?

108
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:15,440
Who gave birth to this AI?

109
00:11:15,694 --> 00:11:25,200
This should be like mathematics that has been developed in the past 5,000 years, also physics, diffusion equations, heat equations.

110
00:11:25,550 --> 00:11:32,900
So I think human dignity and human involvement will be still important, and we have to put more emphasis on it.

111
00:11:33,646 --> 00:11:37,984
So that's my worry, suggestion, and hope to steer development.

112
00:11:38,464 --> 00:11:39,044
Thank you, Sam.

113
00:11:39,264 --> 00:11:39,904
Thank you.

114
00:11:40,366 --> 00:11:47,104
So when it comes to humans in the loop or not, we could talk a little bit about the rise of autonomous laboratories.

115
00:11:47,758 --> 00:11:53,264
Take one, you've been interested in that, and I'd like to hear your thoughts on that too, Craig, because I know you work with a number of drug discovery companies.

116
00:11:54,224 --> 00:12:11,104
one of the, I think, is the hardest research topic recently, not just the material discovery, chemistry and chemical engineering in general is like a so-called self-driving laboratory or autonomous laboratory, which actually combine the conventional like AI tools with uh physical AI.

117
00:12:11,184 --> 00:12:12,464
You know what the physical AI is, right?

118
00:12:12,544 --> 00:12:13,508
That's robotics, right?

119
00:12:13,838 --> 00:12:20,292
So basically what it does is actually the AI is going to propose like uh new experimental process procedures.

120
00:12:20,622 --> 00:12:25,796
But actually, experiment is done by robot systems, automatic robot systems.

121
00:12:26,144 --> 00:12:34,020
And then after getting the data, analyzed data, and feedback to the AI to get the better experimental procedure, better materials.

122
00:12:34,510 --> 00:12:36,900
It's a kind of closed loop, right?

123
00:12:37,134 --> 00:12:40,548
That's going to be really exciting research that's going on.

124
00:12:40,942 --> 00:12:41,636
But you know what?

125
00:12:42,638 --> 00:12:49,988
As like other physical AI systems, one of the limitations is the dexterity of robot systems.

126
00:12:50,670 --> 00:12:55,904
Still, robot cannot do a delicate job as we humans do, right?

127
00:12:56,270 --> 00:13:05,504
So you know the ones that uh like a robot can do something we can do, like our own hand, I think it's that's gonna revolutionize it.

128
00:13:05,584 --> 00:13:10,864
And also robot can, you know, the when it face some kind of unexpected situation.

129
00:13:11,182 --> 00:13:14,852
If robot can handle that kind of things, things done.

130
00:13:15,424 --> 00:13:16,384
Basically, you know.

131
00:13:16,544 --> 00:13:25,124
I mean, in the anonymous two years in discovery, in every era, they're going to revolutionize the science and engineering, technology, whatever.

132
00:13:25,358 --> 00:13:27,344
That's what's going to work in the future.

133
00:13:27,758 --> 00:13:29,284
Is that the future you see, Craig?

134
00:13:31,504 --> 00:13:35,344
it's really hard to predict the future right now.

135
00:13:37,104 --> 00:13:38,624
We're moving so fast.

136
00:13:38,864 --> 00:13:52,544
Um you know, so far I'm very optimistic because um, you know, the there's so much data now, and we need we need that ability to analyze that data.

137
00:13:52,784 --> 00:14:04,464
And um I I you know I think in my field of medical research, we are happy if there's a discovery, no matter where it comes from, if it's going to help patients, right?

138
00:14:04,544 --> 00:14:10,464
I mean, we don't care whether that comes from some human in the loop or not.

139
00:14:10,784 --> 00:14:25,796
Right now, it seems like humans are really, really important still, and that the AI is liberating humans from some of the otherwise maybe even impossible tasks of crunching and analyzing all that data.

140
00:14:26,464 --> 00:14:29,984
So my feeling is so far it looks okay.

141
00:14:31,182 --> 00:14:33,572
where is the motivation going to come from?

142
00:14:33,824 --> 00:14:36,624
Where is the curiosity going to come from?

143
00:14:36,878 --> 00:14:40,544
Are AIs going to start to be curious and motivated on their own?

144
00:14:40,624 --> 00:14:48,900
And maybe then we need to really start to worry because they may have very, very different objectives than we do.

145
00:14:49,184 --> 00:14:55,504
But so far it seems that they are propelling our curiosity forward more rapidly.

146
00:14:56,224 --> 00:15:08,452
And on that motivation question, I mean, it's always struck me with you, Craig, that your motivation is wonder, just pure wonder at the beauty of nature and trying to unravel it.

147
00:15:08,654 --> 00:15:11,908
And that, I guess, Sam, is your motivation for maths.

148
00:15:12,304 --> 00:15:18,500
It's not really finding something to do anything, it's really just understanding, back to your point.

149
00:15:19,182 --> 00:15:23,984
And that is hard to imagine in an AI at the moment, wonder.

150
00:15:24,174 --> 00:15:25,540
But maybe it'll come, as you say.

151
00:15:27,694 --> 00:15:27,968
Eva.

152
00:15:28,688 --> 00:15:41,444
Yes, I'm thinking of we had Geoffrey Hinton with us for the Nobel Prize, and part of the events is that on December 12, the Nobel laureates in physics meet high school students.

153
00:15:42,368 --> 00:15:47,652
And so Geoffrey, he gave a talk and he answered all the questions from the students.

154
00:15:48,302 --> 00:15:54,500
And one thing that he said was that AI is getting awareness.

155
00:15:55,808 --> 00:15:59,492
And I got the impression that that was his concern.

156
00:16:00,368 --> 00:16:10,916
That there is an awareness, and that means that we are having a partner, and I believe that we should make AI our partner to work with us.

157
00:16:13,134 --> 00:16:16,868
But we do have a partner that actually is developing an awareness.

158
00:16:17,568 --> 00:16:20,100
So we need to be working together.

159
00:16:20,686 --> 00:16:26,852
And I also think that we need to be thinking about, of course, the risks, but also thinking in a positive way.

160
00:16:27,502 --> 00:16:36,448
Because I brought in people, we had a discussion at my university, and I brought in people because we had a discussion about AI in education.

161
00:16:36,528 --> 00:16:42,208
And I said, okay, yes, we need to be aware of the risks, but think about what are the positive.

162
00:16:42,638 --> 00:16:45,604
What what how how do we use this in a positive way?

163
00:16:45,998 --> 00:16:54,084
And they came to me afterwards and said, Oh, yes, I thought about this, and now I'm using AI in a new way because this actually got me thinking.

164
00:16:54,734 --> 00:17:00,164
So I think AI is going to be very clever.

165
00:17:01,934 --> 00:17:09,088
I hope that we as human beings do not lose the contact with an experimentalist.

166
00:17:09,408 --> 00:17:13,732
That we don't lose the contact with experiments and what is happening in real life.

167
00:17:14,478 --> 00:17:24,324
We shouldn't only leave it to robots, we should actually have some experience ourselves so that we can interact properly and get it working in a positive direction.

168
00:17:29,088 --> 00:17:37,568
Something that will be talked about later in the day is the human fallibility and the importance of human fallibility in research.

169
00:17:37,870 --> 00:17:42,688
Craig, you like to talk about this often, that the best things are the things that go wrong, right?

170
00:17:44,302 --> 00:17:53,796
Yes, I want to just add though that this idea that AI is developing awareness, I find it fascinating.

171
00:17:55,310 --> 00:17:58,208
and I haven't yet experienced it myself.

172
00:17:59,374 --> 00:18:05,796
when I interact with AI, it seems to be completely devoid of sort of that native curiosity.

173
00:18:07,248 --> 00:18:12,964
and so I wonder whether that's true.

174
00:18:13,230 --> 00:18:17,888
And uh and if it is, then that's a real philosophical issue, right?

175
00:18:18,048 --> 00:18:20,608
Because we've created something that's self-aware.

176
00:18:20,814 --> 00:18:23,492
That's like that's actually like having a child.

177
00:18:24,334 --> 00:18:26,884
That comes with some ethical responsibilities.

178
00:18:27,408 --> 00:18:30,724
And uh so we really all have to pay attention to that.

179
00:18:31,566 --> 00:18:49,488
Um the parents of a potentially pathological child, or is that child actually looking around and saying, oh my goodness, my parents are you know, they're they're crazy.

180
00:18:50,288 --> 00:18:51,568
School children say that.

181
00:18:53,710 --> 00:18:57,796
But I think that's a really profound question.

182
00:18:58,368 --> 00:19:13,888
And you know, the the uh someone said we should be paying attention to the science fiction because there's a huge volumes on what happens with AI, both the dystopias and some incredible utopias that are possible.

183
00:19:14,048 --> 00:19:21,252
And I think if AI is going to become self aware, then that changes everything.

184
00:19:22,062 --> 00:19:26,212
And it really would definitely change the way I think about it.

185
00:19:28,526 --> 00:19:30,768
Sam, you're close to Google DeepMind.

186
00:19:30,848 --> 00:19:34,116
What do you think their opinion is on whether it's becoming self-aware?

187
00:19:36,430 --> 00:19:40,356
I work on a team called Superhuman Reasoning.

188
00:19:41,294 --> 00:19:44,050
So their reasoning capability, at least they dream.

189
00:19:44,288 --> 00:19:56,128
Actually, I realized this some visions of people in science, computer science, that last year they talked about achieving top journal result and millennium problem.

190
00:19:56,654 --> 00:19:58,564
I thought that they are joking or daydreaming.

191
00:19:58,734 --> 00:20:01,892
But it's happening right now, within like six months of their prediction.

192
00:20:02,510 --> 00:20:10,788
And then their reasoning capability will certainly reach, or maybe has already reached, the ability of a human being.

193
00:20:11,246 --> 00:20:20,516
But as Eva correctly pointed out, that what we need to keep is skills, skill set for human race.

194
00:20:21,230 --> 00:20:27,012
So what we are discovering, mathematicians are discovering, is that we are not actually discoverers.

195
00:20:27,342 --> 00:20:29,408
Discovery was not our ultimate goal.

196
00:20:29,488 --> 00:20:31,364
We are rather a way maker.

197
00:20:31,808 --> 00:20:38,148
We need to make and inherit the skills to discover to the next generation.

198
00:20:38,574 --> 00:20:42,404
I think that is something that you might want to keep in mind.

199
00:20:43,310 --> 00:20:47,888
AIs cannot replace us, or even whatever AI company thinks about it.

200
00:20:48,288 --> 00:20:49,488
Thank you very much indeed.

201
00:20:49,568 --> 00:20:51,888
And that is a beautiful point to stop at.

202
00:20:51,968 --> 00:21:00,996
And in fact, you've very cleverly made a segue into the next discussion, which is exactly about what skills define a great scientist in the era of AI.

203
00:21:01,088 --> 00:21:03,008
So thank you all very much indeed.

204
00:21:04,128 --> 00:21:04,688
Thank you.

